  "summary": "The aesthetic tradition of using dramatic black marble with bold white veining from the Basque region of Spain.",
  "description": "Defined by its deep black background with striking white and gray veins, creating high contrast prized in both traditional and contemporary design. The aesthetic developed from the unique geological formations in the Basque quarries and local stonemasonry traditions.",
  "culturalContext": "Nero Marquina — the Basque Country's jet-black marble with white calcite lightning (Markina, Bilbao region) — became modern luxury's black stone: Spanish Baroque church floors, Gaudí-era Barcelona palaces, Art Deco lobbies, and the contemporary \"black marble bathroom\" universal. The white-vein-on-black drama photographs as luxury shorthand; quarries still block-cut the stone, and veining-matching (bookmatching) is the installer's showpiece craft.",
